      "content": "Alveolar fricatives, like /s/ and /z/, are made by forcing air through a narrow channel right behind your upper teeth, near the alveolar ridge. The main difference, as you correctly identified, is the vibration of your vocal cords. For the unvoiced /s/, the airflow is turbulent, but your vocal cords are completely relaxed and not vibrating. In contrast, for the voiced /z/, your vocal cords are actively vibrating as the air passes through the same constriction.",
